TASK 2

Check the Zomato dataset for missing values and outliers in the cost and rating columns. Handle them using pandas and NumPy.

CODE:

# Check missing values
print("Missing values:")
print(df[["cost", "rating"]].isnull().sum())


# Convert columns to numeric
df["cost"] = pd.to_numeric(
    df["cost"],
    errors="coerce"
)

df["rating"] = pd.to_numeric(
    df["rating"],
    errors="coerce"
)


# Fill missing values with median
df["cost"] = df["cost"].fillna(
    df["cost"].median()
)

df["rating"] = df["rating"].fillna(
    df["rating"].median()
)


# Function to find outliers using IQR
def find_outliers(data, column):

    Q1 = data[column].quantile(0.25)
    Q3 = data[column].quantile(0.75)

    IQR = Q3 - Q1

    lower = Q1 - 1.5 * IQR
    upper = Q3 + 1.5 * IQR

    outliers = data[
        (data[column] < lower) |
        (data[column] > upper)
    ]

    return outliers, lower, upper


# Find cost outliers
cost_outliers, cost_lower, cost_upper = find_outliers(
    df,
    "cost"
)

print("\nNumber of cost outliers:",
      len(cost_outliers))


# Find rating outliers
rating_outliers, rating_lower, rating_upper = find_outliers(
    df,
    "rating"
)

print("Number of rating outliers:",
      len(rating_outliers))


# Remove outliers
df = df[
    (df["cost"] >= cost_lower) &
    (df["cost"] <= cost_upper) &
    (df["rating"] >= rating_lower) &
    (df["rating"] <= rating_upper)
]

print("\nDataset shape after removing outliers:")
print(df.shape)


EXPLANATION:

Missing cost and rating values are replaced with their median values. The IQR method is then used to identify unusually high or low values, and these outliers are removed from the dataset.


TASK 3

Encode the cuisine and location categorical columns and scale cost and rating using StandardScaler.

CODE:

from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

# Make sure categorical columns are strings
df["cuisine"] = df["cuisine"].fillna("Unknown")
df["location"] = df["location"].fillna("Unknown")

# One-hot encode categorical columns
encoder = OneHotEncoder(
    handle_unknown="ignore",
    sparse_output=False
)

encoded_data = encoder.fit_transform(
    df[["cuisine", "location"]]
)

# Convert encoded data to DataFrame
encoded_df = pd.DataFrame(
    encoded_data,
    columns=encoder.get_feature_names_out(
        ["cuisine", "location"]
    ),
    index=df.index
)

# Scale cost and rating
scaler = StandardScaler()

scaled_data = scaler.fit_transform(
    df[["cost", "rating"]]
)

scaled_df = pd.DataFrame(
    scaled_data,
    columns=["cost_scaled", "rating_scaled"],
    index=df.index
)

# Combine all processed data
processed_df = pd.concat(
    [
        scaled_df,
        encoded_df
    ],
    axis=1
)

print("PROCESSED DATA:")
print(processed_df.head())


EXPLANATION:

One-hot encoding converts categorical values into numerical columns. StandardScaler changes the cost and rating values so that they have approximately mean 0 and standard deviation 1.


